<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pont du Gard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pont du Gard is a three-tiered Roman aqueduct bridge over the Gardon river in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, built in the 1st century CE to carry water some 50 km to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nemausus</a> (Nîmes). About 49 metres high, it is the tallest of all Roman aqueduct bridges and one of the best preserved, and it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pont-du-gard.jpg" alt="Pont du Gard" width="320" height="123" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benh LIEU SONG (Flickr) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pont_du_Gard_BLS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nemausus</a> was awkwardly placed for a water supply. Plains to its south and east lay too low for water to flow to the city, and the hills to the west were too difficult for engineers, so the only real alternative was the springs of the Fontaine d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eure">Eure</a> near Ucetia (Uzès). The straight-line distance is only about 20 km, but the aqueduct winds for around 50 km to get round the Garrigues de Nîmes, since a tunnel through them would have been 8 to 10 kilometres long.</p><p>The spring lies only 17 m above the distribution basin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimes">Nîmes</a>, giving an average gradient of about 1 in 3,000. Newer excavations suggest construction took place between 40 and 60 CE: coins found in the outflow at Nîmes are no older than the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> (41–54 CE). It is believed to have taken about fifteen years and 800 to 1,000 workers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The bridge is 274 m long, with three recessed levels of arches whose main piers stand in line one above another. Its width varies from 9 m at the bottom to 3 m at the top. It was built largely without mortar or clamps, from an estimated 50,400 tons of local shelly limestone; some blocks weigh up to 6 tons. Many blocks carry numbers and inscriptions such as fronte dextra, guiding the builders to their places.</p><p>The water conduit runs along the top. Its walls are dressed masonry and its floor concrete, covered with a stucco containing shards of pottery and tile and sealed with maltha, a mixture of slaked lime, pork grease and the juice of unripe figs. The aqueduct is estimated to have supplied about 40,000 cubic metres of water a day, which took nearly 27 hours to reach the city. Constant cleaning by workers called circitores was needed to remove mineral deposits and roots.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Maintenance was neglected from the 4th century, and the aqueduct had probably gone out of use by about the 6th century. The bridge survived largely because it served as a toll bridge: in the 13th century the French king granted the seigneurs of Uzès the right to levy tolls, which later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a> of Uzès, who were responsible for its upkeep. In the 1620s the Duke of Rohan had one side of the second row of arches cut back to move his artillery across, weakening it severely.</p><p>A road bridge by the engineer Henri Pitot was added in 1743–47, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> backed repairs carried out between 1855 and 1858. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a>, visiting in 1738, wrote that it was the only time in his life that an object surpassed his expectation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed the site in 1985, and from 1996 a four-year project removed traffic and built a visitor centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pont du Gard” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nemausus became a Latin colony with rich monuments, including the Maison Carrée and the aqueduct carried by the <b>Pont du Gard</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/volcae-arecomici">Volcae Arecomici</a></li></ul></section>
